AIP - a programming language designed for AI (early prototype)

One function per file, no imports, effects in the signature, a generated
one-line-per-function index. See compiler/README.md for what works so far.

RUN IT LOCALLY (Windows)

Needs: the .NET 10 SDK (check with: dotnet --version). Nothing else to install.

Open PowerShell in this folder, then:

  1. Run Hello World (compiles the compiler on first use, then the program):
       scripts\aip.ps1 run examples\hello
     prints: Hello, World!

  2. Or build it, then start the program yourself:
       scripts\aip.ps1 build examples\hello
       examples\hello\build\bin\windows-x64\hello.exe

  3. Check without building, and print the index:
       scripts\aip.ps1 check examples\hello
       scripts\aip.ps1 index examples\hello

  3b. Write a file to disk (creates greeting.txt in the current folder):
       scriptsip.ps1 run exampleswritefile

  4. Build for Linux from Windows (run the result on a Linux machine):
       scripts\aip.ps1 build examples\hello --target linux-x64

  5. Run the tests:
       scripts\test.ps1

If PowerShell says scripts are disabled, or you are in cmd.exe, prefix with:
  powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File scripts\aip.ps1 run examples\hello

MAKE YOUR OWN PROGRAM

Create a folder with a main.fn (copy examples\hello), one function per file,
file name = function name. Then: scripts\aip.ps1 run <your folder>

FOLDERS

  compiler\   the aip compiler (C#) and its standard library (std\*.fn)
  examples\   programs that compile and run
  tests\      projects the compiler must reject, with the expected error
  mock\       hand-written design samples; not compiled yet
  scripts\    aip.ps1 (compiler), test.ps1 (tests), evidence helpers
